    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Detailed knowledge of the characteristics of faults and fractures within carbonate units is crucial for the efficient development of carbonate hydrocarbon reservoirs. We have examined carbonate units within a rifted fault block located east of the Gulf of Suez, Egypt, in order to characterize the distribution, orientation, and internal architecture of features within the block. Three types of structures were documented - fractures, faults, and pressure solution surfaces.\r\n\r\nDistribution of faults and fractures within the block shows a distinct clustering into two groups located near the block-bounding normal faults. Orientations of these features differs from those of the block-bounding faults by up to 60°. A systematic change in orientation of fractures from one side of the block to the other is also noted. The relationship between the block-bounding faults and the internal structures of the block is uncertain. However, the systematic differences highlight the importance of detailed study of structures within rift blocks to the accurate characterization of reservoirs in such tectonic settings.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
